.TH CBMAP_CREATE_FULL 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_create_full \- create a BST map with full customization
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap cbmap_create_full(ccol_data_type key_type,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
                        ccol_comparison_proc_t custom_comparison_proc,
                        char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a self-balancing BST map and gives the caller full control over
the key type, the memory management and the comparison function.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
key_type                 Type of the keys that the map stores
mmgmt_procs              Custom memory management, or NULL
custom_comparison_proc   Custom comparison function, or NULL
err                      Optional pointer that receives an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to the new BST map on success, NULL on failure.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The default comparison for each key type is:
  ccol_char: the platform's native char comparison, signed or unsigned
  to match how char behaves on this platform.
  ccol_signed_char and short/int/long/long_long: a signed comparison,
  which doesn't depend on whether char is signed on this platform.
  ccol_signed_char is a scalar signed char or its typedef int8_t; a
  signed char * string key is ccol_string instead.
  The unsigned integer types and ccol_pointer: an unsigned comparison.
  ccol_float/double/long_double: a numeric floating-point comparison, in
  which a NaN key sorts above every non-NaN key and equal to every other
  NaN key.
  ccol_string: a lexicographic order equivalent to strcmp.
  ccol_other_types (a struct key type, for example): a raw memcmp of the
  representation. That compares indeterminate padding bytes and compares
  each pointer member by its address, not by the object it points at, so
  pass a custom_comparison_proc for a struct key.

All of these compare two keys of the same stored size. Only a key type of
variable width, ccol_string or ccol_other_types, can have two keys of
different sizes, and for those the map compares the common prefix first;
when the prefix is equal it compares the sizes and the shorter key comes
first. Every key of a fixed-width key type is exactly
ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size(3) bytes wide, and every entry point that
takes a key_pair needs that exact width. The same holds with a
custom_comparison_proc, since that function gets two bare key pointers
and no sizes and so reads a width that the key type fixes.

A custom comparison gets void* pointers to the key data, and a key that
comes in through the raw layer can be at any address. Read a key wider
than one byte with memcpy, not through a pointer cast to the key type.
.fi
